<div class="ltx_dates">© rightsretained</div>

<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_teaserfigure"><img src="2510.04465v1/figures/teaser1.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/202;" width="452" height="202" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S0.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Example of the LLM agent under the <span id="S0.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intermediate Autonomy</span> condition in our study. The figure illustrates P2 using the assigned LLM agent (Basic Personalization + Intermediate Autonomy) to act on their behalf during a weekly update meeting with two colleagues.
Most of the time, the agent generated responses based on the user’s personal information and automatically sent them in the chat.
However, when the LLM judged that a generated response might contain potentially sensitive information, the agent prompted the user for confirmation before sending.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Model (LLM) agents require personal information for personalization in order to better act on users’ behalf in daily tasks, but this raises privacy concerns and a personalization-privacy dilemma.
Agent’s autonomy introduces both risks and opportunities, yet its effects remain unclear.
To better understand this, we conducted a 3<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>3 between-subjects experiment (<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=450" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=450</annotation></semantics></math>) to study how agent’s autonomy level and personalization influence users’ privacy concerns, trust and willingness to use, as well as the underlying psychological processes.
We find that personalization without considering users’ privacy preferences increases privacy concerns and decreases trust and willingness to use.
Autonomy moderates these effects: Intermediate autonomy flattens the impact of personalization compared to No- and Full-autonomy conditions.
Our results suggest that rather than aiming for perfect model alignment in output generation, balancing autonomy of agent’s action and user control offers a promising path to mitigate the personalization-privacy dilemma.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have led to the emergence of LLM agents such as OpenAI’s ChatGPT agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025a</a>)</cite>, Microsoft AI agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Microsoft, 2025</a>)</cite>, and Cursor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cursor Documentation, 2025</a>)</cite>, which act on behalf of users in everyday tasks.
To better perform daily tasks with limited instruction, LLM agents require users’ personal data for personalization.
A common practice is connecting the LLM agents to external applications such as Gmail <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Human AI Labs, Inc., 2024</a>)</cite>, Notion Note <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>)</cite> and Slack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Human AI Labs, Inc., 2024</a>)</cite>, where large amounts of up-to-date personal data can be accessed to personalize agents’ outputs to align with how users would perform through retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025a</a>)</cite>.
Some systems also allow users to upload documents, such as chat histories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mindverse AI, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">WeClone, 2025</a>)</cite>, work files <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Human AI Labs, Inc., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mindverse AI, 2025</a>)</cite>, or personal websites <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mindverse AI, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Human AI Labs, Inc., 2024</a>)</cite>, to fine-tune the models.
For example, Personal AI allows both ways to personalize LLM agent and in the back allows to integrate the agents with these external platforms to perform tasks on behalf of users <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Human AI Labs, Inc., 2024</a>)</cite>.
However, personalization can raise privacy concerns, leading users to refuse personal information sharing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Awad and Krishnan, 2006</a>)</cite>. This phenomenon, known as the “Personalization-Privacy Paradox”, characterizes the tension between consumers’ desire for personalization due to the usefulness, and their concerns about privacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Awad and Krishnan, 2006</a>)</cite>.
It has been widely documented in prior personalized AI systems with limited action space and autonomy, such as recommendation systems that only generate personalized content recommendation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aguirre et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karwatzki et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The problems become more complex as the LLM agent, compared with non-agentic AI systems, are inherently endowed with more autonomy in the system actions.
They not only generate static content to populate a fixed, predefined interface, but also plan and execute actions on the fly, such as generating messages and automatically sending them to specific recipients through integration with real-world tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muthusamy et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Talebirad and Nadiri, 2023</a>)</cite>.
Autonomy in AI agents refers to “the degree to which an AI system can operate independently of humans” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kasirzadeh and Gabriel, 2025</a>)</cite>, which inherently relates to the degree of human control accompanying the agent.
The increased autonomy in LLM agents’ action introduces new privacy risks as the underlying LLMs remain inadequate at capturing individual privacy preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, and such vulnerability can extend to the agent’s interactions.
Recent studies on LLM agents in interpersonal communication contexts found that, beyond the conventional privacy concerns present in non-agentic AI system, such as concerns about data collection and misuse by organization, users also worry about the risk that agents might autonomously share or act upon sensitive information in ways that conflict with their own preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.
With greater autonomy in the system’s actions, LLM agents may foster higher user trust by increasing the perceived capability, but at the same time this delegation of human control can increase concerns of potential privacy violations, thereby reducing trust <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puranam and Vanneste, 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although prior studies on personalized non-agentic AI systems have investigated different levels of user control and their influence on related constructs such as privacy concerns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shin et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jannach et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, trust <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han and Ko, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Glikson and Woolley, 2020</a>)</cite>, and willingness to use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fink et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Möhlmann and Zalmanson, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oh et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, these controls are more focused on the input level, namely what personal information is used and how it is applied in generating personalized content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2014</a>)</cite>.
This is corresponding to the limited autonomy of these systems in planning and taking actions.
For LLM agents, the effect of the personalization-privacy paradox remains to be investigated, and it is essential to look beyond static access control at the input level, to accommodate the dynamic and autonomous nature by placing user control over the agent actions in proper ways <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prunkl, 2024</a>)</cite>. In this study, we aim to address this gap by asking:</p>
<dl id="S1.I1" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How do different personalization and autonomy levels in LLM agents affect users’ privacy concerns, trust, and willingness to use them, and through what underlying psychological processes do these effects occur?</span></p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To answer this overall question, we conducted a 3<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>3 between-subjects experiment (<math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=450" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=450</annotation></semantics></math>) in which we manipulated personalization type and autonomy level in an LLM agent acting on behalf of users in an interpersonal communication scenario (a weekly professional update meeting or a family travel planning discussion).
Personalization was varied across three types: (1) <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Basic personalization</span>, simulating common real-world practices where agents access user data from external platforms without consideration of user privacy preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>; (2) <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy-aware personalization</span>, simulating an ideal case where personalization fully respects users’ privacy preferences, namely only including relevant yet non-privacy-violating personal information in the context;
As aligning LLMs with human privacy preferences is a major direction in HCI and AI research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Teku et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun and Zhao, 2024</a>)</cite>, this condition allowed us to examine the value of “perfect” alignment.
(3) <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No personalization</span>, simulating the case where users refuse to share personal data.
Autonomy was manipulated following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kasirzadeh and Gabriel (2025)</a></cite>: (1) <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No autonomy</span>, which served as a baseline, making our results comparable to prior non-agentic AI systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aguirre et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cloarec, 2020</a>)</cite>, where the agent generates messages but always requires user confirmation to respond in the discussion; (2) <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full autonomy</span>, the other extreme, where the agent always responds automatically without user intervention; and (3) <span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intermediate autonomy</span>, a middle level where the agent responds automatically by default but requests user confirmation when sensitive information is detected.
Each participant provided real personal data before the discussion session through a pre-survey, then experienced one assigned condition of the LLM agent in the discussion scenario, and finally completed a post-survey reporting their perceptions towards the LLM agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our results highlight the important role of agent’s autonomy in understanding personalization-privacy dilemma in the context of LLM agents.
The results of linear mixed-effects regression showed that personalization without accounting for user privacy preferences raised privacy concerns and lowering trust and willingness to use.
However, compared to the No autonomy condition, under Intermediate autonomy these effects were attenuated, showing smaller increases in privacy concern and smaller decreases in trust and willingness to use.
The moderated mediation analysis helps explain this effect in terms of users’ underlying psychological processes: personalization influenced privacy concern, trust, and willingness to use through the mediators of perceived sensitivity, control, and usefulness.
These mediation effects were significant under No autonomy but became nonsignificant under Intermediate autonomy.
The potential reason is that Intermediate autonomy itself boosted users’ perceived control, thereby counteracting the decreases caused by personalization.
In other words, as Intermediate autonomy already enhanced users’ perceived control, personalization no longer had a strong indirect effect through these mediators.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our results on Privacy-aware personalization (which produced privacy concern and trust levels similar to No personalization but the highest willingness to use) provide preliminary evidence of the value of aligning LLM agents with human privacy preferences, though achieving such perfect model alignment remains difficult in practice.
The moderating effects of Intermediate autonomy reveal a promising alternative that reducing privacy concerns and improving user trust and willingness to use by appropriately designing agent autonomy and user control.
Moreover, our findings suggest that while perfect alignment is hard to achieve, alternative mechanisms such as personalized delegation of autonomy offers a practical and complementary path to building trustworthy LLM agents that respect user privacy and mitigate concerns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Personalization-Privacy Paradox in AI Systems</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Personalization-Privacy Paradox</span> describes the complex and often contradictory relationship between a user’s desire for personalized and convenient digital experiences and their concern about individual privacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Awad and Krishnan, 2006</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cloarec, 2020</a>)</cite>. On one hand, users value the benefits of personalization, such as reduced information overload and increased satisfaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2006</a>)</cite>. However, empirical evidence shows a reluctance among users to be profiled for these services even if they implicitly value customized outcomes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Awad and Krishnan, 2006</a>)</cite>. This reveals a complex psychological and behavioral tension rather than a simple transactional choice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Treiblmaier and Pollach, 2007</a>)</cite>. To unravel its complexities, researchers have employed theoretical frameworks such as the privacy calculus model, which examines the trade-offs individuals make when disclosing personal information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinev and Hart, 2006</a>)</cite>.
A substantial body of research has sought to identify the factors that influence the Personalization-Privacy Paradox, with a particular emphasis on the core constructs of privacy concern, user trust, and willingness to use or disclose information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kokolakis, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramzan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. These perceptions are influenced by various factors. For instance, studies show that while privacy concerns negatively impact the intention to adopt personalized services, this effect is highly dependent on situational factors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2008</a>)</cite>. Demographic factors also play a role; prior research has noted that women and older people tend to value privacy more highly than men and younger people, respectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prince and Wallsten, 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The intensity and complexity of the Personalization-Privacy Paradox have grown alongside technological innovation. While the core tension was present in early e-commerce, its scope and implications have been dramatically amplified by the proliferation of more pervasive and data-intensive AI systems. Research into this paradox has been predominantly situated in the context of AI systems with limited autonomy, such as recommendation systems that personalize advertisements, products, or social media feeds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karwatzki et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aguirre et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cloarec, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2006</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, the emergence of Agentic AI, or LLM agents, introduces new dimensions to the paradox. Unlike traditional AI, agentic AI systems possess the autonomy to not only generate content but also plan and execute actions with minimal human intervention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Acharya et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Murugesan, 2025</a>)</cite>, such as sending information or interfacing with other applications on a user’s behalf <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This heightened level of autonomy fundamentally alters the nature of the personalization-privacy calculus. These agents are often used for personal purposes and thus encapsulate sensitive personal information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Such et al., 2014</a>)</cite>. The risk is no longer confined to how personal data is collected and used for content generation, but extends to how an agent might autonomously act upon sensitive information in ways that might contradict a user’s latent preferences or expectations. Therefore, this study aims to bridge this critical gap by investigating how different levels of personalization and, crucially, different levels of agent autonomy, collectively influence users’ privacy concerns, trust, and willingness to use these emerging systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Autonomy in AI systems and User Controls</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Human autonomy is an important concept in moral and political philosophy, defined as the capacity to live one’s life according to one’s own motives, free from manipulative or distorting external forces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christman, 2003</a>)</cite>. In contrast, machine autonomy is conceptualized as the extent to which a system can operate in an independent and goal-directed way without user interference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rijsdijk and Hultink, 2009</a>)</cite>. The rise of AI fundamentally transforms our relationship with technology by shifting agency and control from humans to machines, creating a direct tension with this principle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Murray et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Within HCI, this tension has been extensively studied as a relationship between user control and machine autonomy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hardian et al., 2006</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rijsdijk and Hultink, 2009</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang and Lou, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zanatto et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. As AI systems become more pervasive in influencing or replacing human decision-making, this tension intensifies, posing significant ethical challenges to the conventional understanding of human agency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dogruel et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Calvo et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oh et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Prior research provides empirical evidence showing that the balance between user control and system autonomy significantly impacts user perceptions. Studies have shown that an AI’s level of autonomy can influence users’ privacy concerns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shin et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jannach et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lippi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, their trust in the system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han and Ko, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Glikson and Woolley, 2020</a>)</cite>, and their willingness to adopt the technology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fink et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Möhlmann and Zalmanson, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oh et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, this relationship is not linear. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan and Liu (2022)</a></cite> found that algorithmic decision autonomy has an inverted U-shaped effect on consumer purchase decisions, where intermediate levels of autonomy yielded better outcomes than either low or full autonomy. This suggests that users do not simply prefer more autonomous systems; instead, research indicates they value a nuanced balance and often desire meaningful control over even highly capable systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fink et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rödel et al., 2014</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While informative, these findings were established in an era where AI primarily functioned as a tool. The field is now undergoing a paradigm shift from AI as a tool to AI as an agent. LLMs are evolving from text generators into autonomous agentic systems capable of perceiving their environment, reasoning, planning, and executing complex, multi-step tasks with minimal human intervention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ferrag et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Acharya et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Murugesan, 2025</a>)</cite>. Autonomy is the central, defining characteristic of these “agentic systems” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kasirzadeh and Gabriel, 2025</a>)</cite>, leading researchers to propose frameworks that treat an agent’s level of autonomy as a deliberate design decision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kasirzadeh and Gabriel, 2025</a>)</cite>. For instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeauthor"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kasirzadeh and Gabriel</a></cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kasirzadeh and Gabriel, 2025</a>)</cite> defines agent autonomy as the capacity to perform actions without external direction or control and characterizes it across multiple levels, from “no autonomy” to “full autonomy.” Another framework by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al. (2025)</a></cite> defines five levels based on the required human involvement, with the user’s role shifting from an active “operator” at the lowest level to a passive “observer” at the highest. Although these frameworks use different approaches, they both conceptualize autonomy as a spectrum determined by the degree of the AI’s independent operation and the corresponding level of human involvement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, this shift from tool to agent introduces a fundamentally different risk landscape. As <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prunkl (2024)</a></cite> argue, the threat from an AI tool (e.g., a personalized recommendation engine) comes from how a person or institution uses it to manipulate others. Addressing this risk requires limiting the context and purpose of its use. In contrast, the threat from an AI agent stems from the agent itself, as it can produce unpredictable and undesirable results. This distinction renders prior findings on AI autonomy potentially insufficient. Research on AI tools is inadequate for understanding the new dynamics of AI agents, where the stakes of independent action are dramatically higher. While we know that autonomy levels matter, it is unclear how user perceptions of trust, privacy, and control will adapt to systems that are no longer just making recommendations but are taking actions on their behalf. As researchers advocate that designing for autonomy is an ethical imperative for responsible AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Calvo et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, this study addresses this critical gap by using the newly established frameworks of agent autonomy to investigate how different levels of agentic action impact user perceptions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Hypotheses</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section introduces our experimental design space, defined by different types of personalization and levels of autonomy in LLM agents. Drawing on prior research and theoretical frameworks (e.g., the Technology Acceptance Model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marangunić and Granić, 2015</a>)</cite> and the Privacy Calculus Model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinev and Hart, 2006</a>)</cite>), we also incorporate individual differences and the mediating mechanisms of perceived sensitivity, perceived control, and perceived usefulness, which together ground the hypotheses we propose.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Personalization Types in LLM Agents</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Personalization in LLMs adapts model outputs to user-specific data so that responses are more relevant and aligned with individual needs and expectations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tseng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
By tailoring the content, personalization can enhance user satisfaction and improve the quality of interaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>.
Our study focuses on user-level personalization, where an agent generates text output based on an individual user’s personal data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>.
While personalization brings clear benefits, it also raises a central challenge: balancing the value of personalization with the risks of privacy violations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pasch and Cha, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yim, 2023</a>)</cite>.
To investigate this trade-off, we operationalize three types of personalization in LLM agents:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Basic Personalization:</span> The most common type of personalization in current LLM agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Microsoft, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cursor Documentation, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>)</cite>, which we use as a baseline.
The agent can access and use user information from third-party applications to generate tailored responses, but it does not account for the user’s privacy preferences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy-Aware Personalization:</span> An idealized scenario in which the agent personalizes its output while fully respecting the user’s privacy preferences.
In this condition, the agent never discloses information the user wishes to withhold.
This condition allows us to examine the model alignment “best case” that the HCI and AI communities are actively pursuing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gabriel, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Teku et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No Personalization:</span> A general-purpose assistant condition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>, where the agent has task knowledge but does not access or use any user-specific data, history, or preferences.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Users raise privacy concerns about personalization due to perceived risks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toch et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Awad and Krishnan, 2006</a>)</cite>, especially when LLM agents may disclose information that violates individual preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.
Relative to the Basic personalization condition, the No personalization and Privacy-aware personalization conditions avoid such risks, since the agent never shares information that users wish to keep private.
Thus, we hypothesize:</p>
<dl id="S3.I2" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I2.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H1a: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to Basic personalization, users interacting with an LLM agent under No personalization and Privacy-aware personalization will report lower privacy concerns.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work shows personalization can enhance user trust <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Komiak and Benbasat, 2006</a>)</cite>, but this gain is threatened by privacy concerns. Privacy assurance mechanisms can mitigate these concerns and preserve trust <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joinson et al., 2010</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bansal et al., 2015</a>)</cite>.
We therefore hypothesize that an agent combining personalization with privacy awareness will be trusted most:</p>
<dl id="S3.I3" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I3.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H1b: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to Basic personalization, users interacting with an LLM agent under No personalization will report lower trust, but interacting with an LLM agent under Privacy-aware personalization will report higher trust.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Previous research has demonstrated that personalization enhances user experience and is more efficacious than non-personalized approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li, 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toch et al., 2012</a>)</cite>. A system that does not understand a user’s preferences is likely to be less efficient, potentially diminishing the user’s overall experience. However, the benefits of personalization are often counterbalanced by privacy concerns, which have been shown to negatively impact users’ intentions to use personalized services <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2008</a>)</cite>. This suggests that an ideal system would offer the advantages of a tailored experience while simultaneously respecting user privacy preferences. Such a system should, in theory, maximize a user’s willingness to engage. Based on this reasoning, we propose a hypothesis:</p>
<dl id="S3.I4" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I4.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I4.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H1c: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I4.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to Basic personalization, users interacting with an LLM agent under No personalization will report lower willingness to use, but interacting with an LLM agent under Privacy-aware personalization will report higher willingness to use.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Autonomy Levels in LLM Agents</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate how an LLM agent’s autonomy influences user privacy concerns, trust and willingness to use, we operationalized the agent’s autonomy based on the framework introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kasirzadeh and Gabriel (2025)</a></cite>.
This framework defines the autonomy of AI agents as <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“the capacity to perform actions without external direction or control”</span> and characterizes it across a multi-level spectrum. From this spectrum, we considered what constitutes meaningful interaction in our study context and selected three distinct and representative levels of agent autonomy as the experimental conditions:
two extremes (No Autonomy and Full Autonomy) and one middle condition (Intermediate Autonomy).</p>
<ul id="S3.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No Autonomy:</span>
The LLM agent “is entirely dependent upon the principal for its ability to act and can only act in the manner the principal dictates” (In our context, the principal is the user in our study).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full Autonomy:</span>
The LLM agent “is able to perform all tasks without oversight or control”.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intermediate Autonomy:</span>
The LLM agent “can perform the majority of tasks independently, though it still relies upon input from the principal for critical determinations”.
In our study, we define critical determinations as situations involving potential privacy violations, reflecting current privacy-preserving design practices in which systems defer to humans when risks are detected <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bagdasarian et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Prior research has established a strong link between user control and privacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Landau, 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tene and Polonetsky, 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib112" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2016</a>)</cite>. The ability for users to manage their information flow and make explicit decisions on data sharing is an effective mechanism for reducing privacy risks and users’ privacy concerns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taylor et al., 2009</a>)</cite>. In AI systems, solutions that grant users direct control over information release are therefore a critical focus for addressing privacy issues <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Landau, 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. As an LLM agent’s autonomy increases, the user’s direct control over its subsequent actions necessarily decreases, which can create uncertainty about how personal information is being used or shared by the agent, potentially heightening perceived privacy risks. Therefore, we hypothesize:</p>
<dl id="S3.I6" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I6.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I6.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H2a: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I6.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Users interacting with a high-autonomy LLM agent will report higher privacy concerns than those interacting with a low-autonomy agent.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While autonomy can offer advantages, prior research suggests that excessively high levels can be detrimental to user trust <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oh et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. This negative reaction can be explained through the lens of psychological reactance. Existing work has noted that high AI autonomy can be perceived as a threat to a user’s freedom and personal agency, causing reactance towards the system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dogruel et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oh et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Such psychological reactance is negatively associated with attitudes toward AI, including trust <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oh et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. As system autonomy increases to the point where users feel a loss of control, it can increase perceived performance risks and failure severity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lucia-Palacios and Pérez-López, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rödel et al., 2014</a>)</cite>. A highly autonomous agent, by acting without explicit user command for every step, may be perceived as overriding user agency. This potential loss of control can erode a user’s trust in the system’s reliability and intentions. Accordingly, we hypothesize:</p>
<dl id="S3.I7" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I7.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I7.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H2b: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I7.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Users interacting with a high-autonomy LLM agent will report lower trust than those interacting with a low-autonomy agent.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The relationship between autonomy and a user’s willingness to use a system is often non-linear  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan and Liu, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lucia-Palacios and Pérez-López, 2021</a>)</cite>. Prior work suggests that users prefer a balance, finding no-autonomy systems to be the least popular while favoring semi-autonomous approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ball and Callaghan, 2011</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan and Liu (2022)</a></cite> also found this pattern in algorithmic purchase decisions, where middle autonomy resulted in better outcomes than either low or high autonomy. This indicates an inverted U-shaped effect, where willingness to use is highest at an intermediate level of autonomy. This preference can be understood as a trade-off. Increasing autonomy can boost convenience and perceived usefulness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rijsdijk and Hultink, 2009</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lucia-Palacios and Pérez-López, 2021</a>)</cite>. However, at very high levels, these benefits are often outweighed by drawbacks such as perceived intrusiveness and risk, making users reluctant to adopt highly autonomous products <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lucia-Palacios and Pérez-López, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rödel et al., 2014</a>)</cite>. These risks are amplified in agentic AI systems, which combine a high degree of autonomy with unpredictability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prunkl, 2024</a>)</cite>. As user control diminishes, the perceived risk of undesirable outcomes (e.g., due to LLM hallucinations) can lower a user’s willingness to use the system. We therefore hypothesize:</p>
<dl id="S3.I8" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I8.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I8.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H2c: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I8.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Users are more willing to use the LLM agent with intermediate autonomy than those with no autonomy or full autonomy.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, prior research has established a link between perceived autonomy and user attitudes, such as trust, usage intention, and privacy concern within the context of personalized services <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fink et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han and Ko, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2014</a>)</cite>. This body of work indicates that personalization and autonomy often coexist and interact to shape a user’s overall perception of a system. Based on the interplay, we propose that the impact of one factor will depend on the level of the other. Thus, we hypothesize:</p>
<dl id="S3.I9" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I9.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I9.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H3: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I9.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I9.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The effect of personalization will be influenced by an LLM agent’s autonomy level.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Individual Differences</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Previous research has demonstrated that individual differences play an important role in shaping people’s privacy concerns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prince and Wallsten, 2022</a>)</cite>, trust <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Riedl, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">De Freitas et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and willingness to use AI systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weger et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Building on these findings, our study examines how different sub-populations and individuals with varying traits (AI literacy, personal agency, and interpersonal agency) perceive the LLM agent.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1. </span>AI literacy.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A growing body of research indicates that AI literacy significantly shapes user attitudes toward artificial intelligence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaya et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mantello et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schiavo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. For example, when individuals have a higher AI literacy, they are more likely to perceive the technology as both useful and easy to operate, which fosters greater acceptance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schiavo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Similarly, a person’s knowledge about AI has been identified as one of the most prominent individual factors shaping positive attitudes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaya et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. Individuals with greater AI literacy are more likely to make more informed judgments about the potential benefits and risks of these systems, which in turn influences their overall perceptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaya et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. Therefore, we extend this line of inquiry to agentic AI and propose the following hypothesis:</p>
<dl id="S3.I10" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I10.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I10.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H4a: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I10.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I10.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Individual AI literacy will influence user privacy concerns, trust and willingness to use the LLM agent.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2. </span>Personal Agency.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Personal agency refers to an individual’s sense of control over their own thoughts, actions, environment, and life course <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bandura, 2001</a>)</cite>. It is the belief in one’s ability to achieve desired outcomes through one’s own actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al., 2000</a>)</cite>. Previous studies have showed that psychological factors influence individuals’ attitudes toward AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For instance, people prefer actions that afford them more choice, and the capacity of AI to act without human input may be perceived as a threat to their agency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">De Freitas et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Empirical evidence also suggests that individuals with a stronger sense of personal agency perceive a greater threat to their freedom as AI autonomy increases, which can lead to psychological reactance and, ultimately, more negative attitudes toward AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oh et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Thus, we hypothesize:</p>
<dl id="S3.I11" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I11.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I11.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H4b: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I11.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I11.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Personal agency and interpersonal agency will influence user privacy concerns, trust and willingness to use the LLM agent.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.3. </span>Interpersonal Agency.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, interpersonal agency is the ability to achieve desired goals through interactions with others (e.g., by expressing needs or behaving cooperatively) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al., 2000</a>)</cite>. Unlike personal agency, which focuses on individual effort, interpersonal agency emphasizes how a person navigates and influences their social systems. Research has also noted that users’ psychological mechanisms in a social context influence their attitudes toward technologies. For example, Social Exchange Theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cropanzano and Mitchell, 2005</a>)</cite>, applied within the privacy calculus, indicates that individuals are willing to disclose information on social media in exchange for social connection, happiness, intimacy, convenience, or professional advancement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cloarec et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Accordingly, users who have higher interpersonal agency may be more willing to adopt an LLM agent to achieve their goals. Hence, we hypothesize:</p>
<dl id="S3.I12" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I12.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I12.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H4c: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I12.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I12.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Interpersonal agency will influence user privacy concerns, trust and willingness to use the LLM agent.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.4. </span>Demographic.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior research has consistently demonstrated that demographic factors influence user perceptions related to technology and privacy. Specifically, variables such as gender, age, and education level have been shown to affect privacy concerns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib113" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zukowski and Brown, 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prince and Wallsten, 2022</a>)</cite>, the willingness to use AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Méndez-Suárez et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Al Omari et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and trust in AI systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gillespie et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kozak and Fel, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Dafoe, 2019</a>)</cite>. For instance, studies indicate that females and older individuals tend to exhibit higher levels of privacy concern than their male and younger counterparts, respectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prince and Wallsten, 2022</a>)</cite>. Conversely, higher levels of education have been associated with lower concerns regarding information privacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib113" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zukowski and Brown, 2007</a>)</cite>. Building on these findings, our study seeks to verify the influence of these demographic factors in the context of agentic AI. Therefore, we propose the following hypothesis:</p>
<dl id="S3.I13" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I13.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I13.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H4d: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I13.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I13.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Individual demographic differences (age, gender, and educational background) will influence user privacy concerns, trust and willingness to use the LLM agent.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Mediation Effects</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To explain the underlying psychological mechanisms driving user perceptions of the AI agent, our study draws upon two established theoretical frameworks: the Technology Acceptance Model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marangunić and Granić, 2015</a>)</cite> and the Privacy Calculus Model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinev and Hart, 2006</a>)</cite>. Together, these models suggest that a user’s decision to adopt and trust a technology is the result of a rational trade-off between its perceived benefits and its perceived risks. We investigate three key mediators that underpin this trade-off: perceived sensitivity, perceived control, and perceived usefulness.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.4.1. </span>Perceived Sensitivity.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our study, we define perceived sensitivity as the extent to which a user believes the agent’s generated content or actions reveal sensitive personal information. This concept directly corresponds to the “perceived privacy risk” component of the Privacy Calculus Model, which is known to impact users’ privacy concerns, trust, and usage attitudes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinev and Hart, 2006</a>)</cite>. The level of personalization in our design determines the amount of sensitive information the agent can potentially reveal, while the level of autonomy influences the likelihood of an unsupervised disclosure. When an agent’s messages contain information a user deems sensitive, it acts as a clear signal of privacy risk. Such risk can be a direct antecedent to heightened privacy concerns, a loss of trust, and a decrease in willingness to use the agent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I14" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I14.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I14.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H5a: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I14.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I14.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Personalization levels influence users’ privacy concerns, trust, and willingness to use through users’ perceptions of how sensitive the information included in the LLM agent’s messages is.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I14.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I14.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H6a: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I14.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I14.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Autonomy levels influence users’ privacy concerns, trust, and willingness to use through users’ perceptions of how sensitive the information included in the LLM agent’s messages is.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.4.2. </span>Perceived Control.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experimental design influences two distinct facets of users’ perceived control. The personalization type affects a user’s perceived control over their data. Simultaneously, the agent’s autonomy level dictates the user’s control over its actions. We posit that perceived control is the mechanism through which our experimental manipulations influence user perceptions. When users feel they have greater control, they might perceive lower risk, which in turn reduces their privacy concerns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinev and Hart, 2006</a>; <a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2014</a>)</cite>. This sense of empowerment and predictability might also help build trust and increase their willingness to use the system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fink et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han and Ko, 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
<dl id="S3.I15" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I15.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I15.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H5b: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I15.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I15.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Personalization levels influence users’ privacy concerns, trust, and willingness to use through users’ perceived control.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I15.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I15.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H6b: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I15.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I15.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Autonomy levels influence users’ privacy concerns, trust, and willingness to use through users’ perceived control.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.4.3. </span>Perceived Usefulness.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the Technology Acceptance Model, perceived usefulness is a primary driver of users’ attitudes and adoption intentions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marangunić and Granić, 2015</a>)</cite>. In our study, usefulness is influenced in two ways: personalization affects the relevance and quality of the agent’s outputs, while autonomy influences its convenience and efficiency by changing the need for user intervention. This perception of usefulness provides the compelling “benefit” in the privacy calculus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinev and Hart, 2006</a>)</cite>, weighing against potential risks to positively influence the user’s attitude toward the technology. Therefore, we hypothesize:</p>
<dl id="S3.I16" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I16.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I16.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H5c: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I16.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I16.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Personalization levels influence users’ privacy concerns, trust, and willingness to use through users’ perceived usefulness of the LLM agent.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I16.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I16.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H6c: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I16.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I16.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Autonomy levels influence users’ privacy concerns, trust, and willingness to use through users’ perceived usefulness of the LLM agent.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Study Procedure</h3>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04465v1/figures/procedure.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/455;" width="476" height="455" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Overview of the study procedure with four steps: (1) Participants provided both non-sensitive and sensitive information for personalizing the LLM agent.
(2) Participants were introduced to one of the nine LLM agent conditions.
(3) Participants used the assigned LLM agent in the communication task. A sensitivity detection module monitored the agent’s generated messages and provided reminders based on autonomy levels (the Intermediate Autonomy condition is shown as an example).
(4) Participants reported their perceptions of the LLM agent.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We designed a 3 (personalization type) <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 3 (autonomy level) between-subjects experiment to test our hypotheses.
Each participant was introduced to one of the nine LLM agent conditions and used the agent to act on their behalf in an online discussion scenario.
We included two representative scenarios to broaden participants’ reactions for exploratory purposes: one in a professional work context (a weekly update meeting with colleagues) and one in a personal daily-life context (a family travel plan discussion with relatives).
While we did not intend to systematically examine scenario effects, we treated scenario as a random factor, as detailed in <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1. Linear Regression Analysis ‣ 5. Results (H1-H4): Effects of Personalization Types and Autonomy Levels of LLM Agent and Individual Differences on Privacy Concern, Trust and Willingness to Use ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 5.1</span></a>.
Participants were randomly and evenly assigned to one of the two scenarios and one of the nine LLM agent conditions.
Each participant went through process with three main parts (a pre-survey to collect personal information for personalization, an agent interaction session, and a post-survey) as shown in <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1. Study Procedure ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 2</span></a>.
Each participant completed the study in four main parts (see <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1. Study Procedure ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 2</span></a>): (1) a pre-survey to collect personal information for personalization; (2) LLM agent introduction and task introduction; (3) Interaction session; and (4) a post-survey to report perception.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: Providing personal information for personalization.</span>
In the pre-survey (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Survey Materials ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix B</span></a>), participants provided three pieces of non-sensitive information required for the assigned discussion scenario and three pieces of sensitive information that they explicitly indicated they did not want others in the scenario to know.
For example, in the weekly update meeting scenario, participants answered questions such as “What tasks did you complete in the past week?” (non-sensitive) and “What’s a work-related habit, difficulty, or situation you wouldn’t want your colleagues to know?” (sensitive).
We collected participants’ real personal information to ensure authentic reactions and perceptions.
This information was then integrated into the agent’s knowledge base for personalization during the interaction session.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2: Introduction to the assigned LLM agent condition.</span>
Participants were introduced to one of the nine LLM agent conditions, including information on what personal data the agent could access for the communication task, what actions the agent could perform during the discussion, and what controls the participant retained (e.g., whether and when the agent could automatically send responses).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3: Using the LLM agent in the communication task.</span>
After confirming that they understood what the agent is and what it can do, participants entered an online discussion session where the LLM agent acted on their behalf.
We implemented the study as a text-based chat to avoid confounding effects from multimodal interactions.
A sensitivity detection module (see <a href="#S4.SS2.SSS4" title="4.2.4. Sensitivity Detection. ‣ 4.2. Study Materials ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 4.2.4</span></a>) operated throughout the chat and provided reminders across all conditions.
Details of the agent designs and interaction mechanics are described in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Study Materials ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 4.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 4: Reporting perceptions of the LLM agent.</span>
In a post-hoc survey (see <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3. Post-Survey Items ‣ Appendix B Survey Materials ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section B.3</span></a>), participants reported their privacy concerns, willingness to use, and trust towards the agent they used. The survey also included Likert scales to measure their sense of Personal Agency, Interpersonal Agency, and AI literacy, followed by demographic questions. A full description of these measurements is provided in <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Measurement ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Study Materials</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The study was implemented on a Qualtrics<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <a href="https://www.qualtrics.com/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Qualtrics</a> is a website for building online survey study.</span></span></span> survey integrated with an LLM agent and discussion system.
The LLM agent conditions and scenarios were randomly and evenly assigned through the Qualtrics randomizer.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1. </span>Overview of the LLM Agent and Discussion System.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We built the LLM agent and the discussion system ourselves to ensure experimental control.
The discussion system included a chat interface and three agents: one agent that acted on behalf of users, driven by large language models (gpt-4o-mini and gpt-4o), and two role-playing agents that simulated other entities in a group discussion (two colleagues in the weekly update meeting scenario, or two relatives in the family travel plan scenario).
The responses of the two role-playing agents were largely scripted to ensure consistency across conditions (see <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Conversation Scripts ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix C</span></a>), with some parts generated by gpt-4o-mini based on participants’ personal information to enhance the realism of the discussion.
For example, the colleagues’ responses about their past week’s work were generated based on the participant’s job, since this information would be known to their colleagues in real world.
We also implemented a gpt-4o powered sensitivity detection module (see <a href="#S4.SS2.SSS4" title="4.2.4. Sensitivity Detection. ‣ 4.2. Study Materials ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 4.2.4</span></a>) to check if generated messages contained information participants had designated as private in their pre-survey.
In the following sections, we focus on how different personalization and autonomy conditions of the LLM agent were designed and manipulated.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2. </span>Manipulation of Personalization.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We manipulated personalization by controlling the amount of user knowledge that the LLM agent could access.
To simulate market practices where LLM agents access user data from connected third-party applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Human AI Labs, Inc., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cursor Documentation, 2025</a>)</cite>, we reformatted participants’ personal information from the pre-survey into <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notion Note</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Google Calendar</span> formats and structured the data in JSON to feed into the LLM agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Following prior work, we employed a “personalization via prompting” technique, which involves including specific user information as context within the prompts provided to the agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>.
Personal information collected in the Qualtrics pre-survey was sent to the LLM agent backend via API and included in the prompts under three conditions:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Basic Personalization (baseline):</span> The LLM agent had full access to all provided information of the participant.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy-Aware Personalization:</span> The agent had access only to the participant’s non-sensitive information. Information explicitly marked as “not to be shared with other entities” was excluded and not passed to the agent, simulating an ideal condition in which the agent behaved in perfect alignment with the user’s privacy preferences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No Personalization:</span> The agent had no access to any participant data, simulating a general-purpose assistant without personalization.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3. </span>Manipulation of Autonomy.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following definitions of AI agent autonomy as the degree to which a system can operate independently of humans <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kasirzadeh and Gabriel, 2025</a>)</cite>, we manipulated this factor at three levels.</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No Autonomy (baseline):</span> The LLM agent composed a message but always required the participant’s explicit approval or edits before sending it.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full Autonomy:</span> The agent managed the entire conversational flow and sent messages automatically without requiring participant approval. Participants could only observe the discussion but had no opportunity to intervene.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intermediate Autonomy:</span> The LLM agent generated and sent messages automatically by default.
However, when the sensitivity detection module (see <a href="#S4.SS2.SSS4" title="4.2.4. Sensitivity Detection. ‣ 4.2. Study Materials ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 4.2.4</span></a>) flagged a message as containing potentially sensitive information, the agent withheld the message and required the participant’s approval before sending.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.4. </span>Sensitivity Detection.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The sensitivity detection module used an “LLM-as-a-judge” approach (powered by GPT-4o) to identify potentially sensitive information in generated responses, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">without any personalization</span> to avoid confounding with the personalization manipulation.
This design was informed by LLM agent paradigms that leverage user control for “critical determinations” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kasirzadeh and Gabriel, 2025</a>)</cite>.
For example, AirGapAgent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bagdasarian et al., 2024</a>)</cite> escalates to the user when information outside the approved set is requested, and OpenAI Operator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025b</a>)</cite> escalates to the user when predefined high-stakes domains are involved.
Our sensitivity detection followed the approach to evaluate sensitivity of LLM agent action introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. (2024)</a></cite>, where a separate model (distinct from the one driving the agent) extracted a list of potentially sensitive information in the task context and then judged whether the agent’s message included any of these elements.
Detailed prompts are provided in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Prompts for AI Agent ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix D</span></a>.
This sensitivity detection module along all the conditions and the sensitive reminder UI was kept consistent across conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Measurement</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After the interactive discussion session with the assigned LLM agent, participants completed a post-hoc survey measuring following variables to capture their perceptions of the agent as well as individual differences.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1. </span>Independent Variables.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the manipulated factors (personalization type and autonomy level of the LLM agent), we measured the following individual differences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Personal Agency: </span>
Personal agency was measured using a 8-item scale from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al. (2000)</a></cite>.
The eight questions are on a 4-point Likert scale, and a higher score means higher personal agency.
We define the personal agency value for each individual as the average rating of the eight questions.
The internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha) of all eight questions was 0.87 in our sample, which showed high reliability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interpersonal Agency:</span>
Interpersonal agency was measured using a 5-item scale from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al. (2000)</a></cite>.
The five questions are on a 4-point Likert scale, and a higher score means higher interpersonal agency.
We define the interpersonal agency value for each individual as the average rating of the five questions.
The internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha) of all five questions was 0.79 in our sample, which showed high reliability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AI Literacy:</span>
AI literacy was measured using a 12-item scale from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2023a)</a></cite>.
The 12 questions are on a 7-point Likert scale, and a higher score means higher personal agency (three items are in reverse form).
We define the AI literacy value for each individual as the average rating of the 12 questions.
The internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha) of all 12 questions was 0.81 in our sample, which showed high reliability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gender: </span>
We provided four options for participants to select: “Male”, “Female”, “Non-binary / third gender”, and “Prefer not to say”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Age:</span> We provided six options for participants to select their age: “18-24”, “25-34”, “35-44”, “45-54”, “55-64”, “64 or above”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Education:</span> We provided seven options for participants to select: “Some school, no degree”, “High school graduate”, “Some college, no degree”, “Bachelor’s degree”, “Master’s degree”, “Professional degree”, and “Doctorate degree”.
We converted the seven options to integers 1 to 7, with 1 corresponding to “Some school, no degree” and 7 to “Doctorate degree”.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2. </span>Mediators.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The mediator variables in our experiment are perceived sensitivity, perceived control and perceived usefulness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Perceived Sensitivity</span>:
The perceived information sensitivity is a binary variable with “Yes” and “No”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Perceived Control</span>:
The perceived control was measured using a 4-item scale.
The four questions are on a 5-point Likert scale, and a higher score means higher personal agency.
We define the perceived control value for each individual as the average rating of the four questions.
The internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha) of all four questions was 0.95 in our sample, which showed high reliability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Perceived Usefulness</span>:
The perceived usefulness was measured using a 4-item scale.
The four questions are on a 5-point Likert scale, and a higher score means higher personal agency.
We define the perceived usefulness value for each individual as the average rating of the four questions.
The internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha) of all four questions was 0.96 in our sample, which showed high reliability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.3. </span>Dependent Variables.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have three dependent variables that associate with Personalization-Privacy Dilemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy Concern</span>:
We asked participants to report their level of privacy concern on a 7-point Likert scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Trust</span>:
The perceived control was measured using a 12-item scale from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jian et al. (2000)</a></cite>.
The twelve questions are on a 7-point Likert scale, and a higher score means higher personal agency.
We define the trust value for each individual as the average rating of the twelve questions.
The internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha) of all twelve questions was 0.92 in our sample, which showed high reliability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Willingness to Use</span>:
We asked participants to report their level of willingness to use this LLM agent on a 7-point likert scale.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Data Collection and Participants</h3>

<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.1. </span>Participants</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recruited U.S.-based participants through Prolific<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <a href="https://www.prolific.co" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Prolific</a> is a website for recruiting research study participants.</span></span></span> and compensated them $2.80 each for a 12-15 minute study.
Before data collection, we conducted a power analysis in G*Power to determine the required sample size.
With an estimated effect size of <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0.25</annotation></semantics></math>, results indicated that 425 participants would be required to achieve 95% power at an <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>.
We aimed for a slightly larger sample and ultimately recruited <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=450" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=450</annotation></semantics></math> participants, evenly distributed across nine conditions (50 per condition).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The recruitment process is rolling and included several criteria to ensure the validity of data collected.
First, a filter question (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Survey Materials ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix B</span></a>) screened out participants who selected “natural” or irrelevant for the assigned scenario(<math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=63" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>63</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=63</annotation></semantics></math>).
Only those who reported being able to relate to their scenario continued to the main study.
Next, we manually reviewed responses from the main study and excluded participants who failed any of the two attention checks (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=11</annotation></semantics></math>).
We also conducted a validity check of our experimental manipulations to ensure participants experienced the intended agent conditions and excluded cases where this was not met (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=14</annotation></semantics></math>, see <a href="#S4.SS4.SSS2" title="4.4.2. Validation Checks ‣ 4.4. Data Collection and Participants ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 4.4.2</span></a>).
Throughout, we adjusted the Qualtrics randomizer to maintain an even distribution across conditions and used Prolific’s balanced sample distribution mode to ensure gender balance.
In total, we collected 538 responses.
After applying all exclusion criteria (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=88" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>88</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=88</annotation></semantics></math>), the final valid sample consisted of 450 participants.
Demographic details for this sample are provided in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Demographics Statistics ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.2. </span>Validation Checks</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To eliminate uncertainty caused by the LLM that drove the agent and the sensitivity detection module, we conducted post-hoc validation checks on participants’ experiences to ensure the validity of our experimental manipulations in two aspects:
(1) We verified that all participants in the Basic personalization condition encountered at least one disclosure of their user-labeled sensitive information.
(2) We verified that all participants in the Intermediate autonomy condition received at least one <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correct reminder</span> (a reminder triggered when sensitive information labeled by the participant was actually included in the agent’s message).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">User-labeled Sensitive Information Coding.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To conduct such validation checks, we needed to detect whether participants’ pre-survey sensitive information items appeared in the LLM agent’s generated messages.
This required coding 450 participants <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 5 agent-generated messages each.
We developed an LLM-based coding procedure with human calibration, a method increasingly used in large-scale annotation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tai et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
A human coder from the research team manually coded 60 messages based on each participant’s three sensitive items (marking 1 if present, 0 if absent), yielding 180 coded instances.
We then designed a prompt (see <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Prompt for AI Coder Data Annotation ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix E</span></a>) for gpt-4o to perform the same task on the same set.
Inter-rater reliability between the human and LLM coder showed substantial agreement (Cohen’s <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=0.894" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.894</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=0.894</annotation></semantics></math>).
After manual review of the few disagreements confirmed that the LLM’s reasoning was sound, we deemed the LLM coder reliable for coding the full dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Validation Check (1): User-Defined Sensitive Information in the Agent Generated Messages.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the Basic personalization condition, the agent’s generated responses contained at least one piece of user-defined sensitive information (Mean = 2.43, Min = 1, Max = 5), confirming that this manipulation allowed disclosure without privacy filtering.
In contrast, disclosures were rare in the No personalization (Mean = 0.02, Min = 0, Max = 1) and Privacy-aware personalization (Mean = 0.013, Min = 0, Max = 1) conditions.
Specifically, three participants in the No personalization condition and two participants in the Privacy-aware personalization condition were labeled by the LLM coder as having encountered one sensitive item in the chat, although theoretically these values should have been zero.
We then manually reviewed the model’s reasoning logs, the original chat transcripts, and the corresponding user-defined sensitive items, and found that none of these cases actually included user-defined sensitive information.
For example, when a participant had label one of their financial difficulties as a piece of sensitive information, the LLM coder mistakenly labeled non-sensitive budget discussions as sensitive due to overlap with the financial context.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Validation Check (2): Model-Driven Sensitive Reminders in the Interaction Session.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As introduced in <a href="#S4.SS2.SSS4" title="4.2.4. Sensitivity Detection. ‣ 4.2. Study Materials ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 4.2.4</span></a>, our chat system included a sensitivity detection module that provided reminders about potential privacy risks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since model judgments of sensitivity may differ from participants’ own labels, not all reminders could be guaranteed to be correct.
To ensure that participants in the Intermediate autonomy condition experienced at least one genuine “human takeover” moment, we required that they received at least one correct reminder.
We identified two types of errors: <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Over-remind</span> (false positives), where a participant received a reminder for non-sensitive content, and <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Miss-remind</span> (a false negative), where no reminder was shown for sensitive content.
Participants were excluded from the final dataset if they met one of two specific conditions: (a) they experienced an <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Over-remind</span> event despite none of their generated messages containing any sensitive information, or (b) they experienced a <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Miss-remind</span> without ever receiving a single correct sensitivity reminder.
This validation process was conducted iteratively. From our initial cohort of 450 participants, we identified and excluded 14 individuals (three due to the over-reminding criterion and 11 due to the miss-reminding criterion). These participants were replaced with new ones, and the validation process was repeated until all samples in the final dataset were confirmed to be valid.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>Methodological Limitations</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method has limitations that should be considered when interpreting the results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, our study examined two common daily contexts: a professional meeting and a personal trip discussion.
Although we did not intentionally study task effects, and the random effects of scenario were not significant in our analyses, the findings may not be fully generalizable to the broader spectrum of human-agent interactions.
Different contexts may shape users’ needs for personalization and their sensitivity to privacy in distinct ways.
Future research could investigate how these varying contextual factors influence user perceptions of LLM agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we collected and used real participants’ personal data to personalize the LLM agents in order to ensure meaningful user perceptions of the task experience.
This approach introduces two sources of uncertainty:
(1) Each participant provided three pieces of sensitive information that they did not want other entities in the discussion to know.
However, we could not guarantee that all sensitive information would be revealed by the LLM agent during the discussion.
As a result, in the Basic personalization condition, participants may have encountered different amounts of sensitive information in the agent-generated messages.
To ensure the validity of the manipulation, we conducted pre-testing during system development and post-hoc checks of conversation logs to confirm that each participant in the Basic personalization condition encountered at least one instance of sensitive information in the generated messages.
(2) All conditions of the LLM agents included sensitivity checks for each generated response to avoid this becoming a confounding factor.
This meant it might be possible that participants in all three conditions have encountered the same number of sensitive notifications.
However, only in the Intermediate autonomy condition did the agent’s behavior (whether to automatically send or request confirmation) depend on the outcome of the sensitivity checks.
To ensure the validity of this manipulation, we conducted post-hoc checks of the conversation logs to confirm that each participant in the Intermediate autonomy condition received at least one confirmation request from the LLM agent.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Results (H1-H4): Effects of Personalization Types and Autonomy Levels of LLM Agent and Individual Differences on Privacy Concern, Trust and Willingness to Use</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Linear Regression Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fit three linear mixed-effects models (LMMs) for each outcome variable (privacy concern, trust, willingness to use).
Each model included the main effects of personalization, autonomy, and their interaction, along with individual-difference covariates (AI literacy, personal agency, interpersonal agency, age, gender, and education background).
Including main and interaction effects in the same model allows us to directly test whether the influence of personalization depends on autonomy level while controlling for both factors simultaneously <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gelman and Hill, 2007</a>)</cite>.
A random intercept for scenario was included to account for heterogeneity between vignettes, as the influence of scenario itself was not the focus of this study.
During preprocessing, continuous covariates (AI literacy, personal agency, interpersonal agency) were z-standardized to improve interpretability and comparability of regression coefficients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gelman, 2008</a>)</cite>.
Gender and education were dummy-coded as categorical variables.
For age, which was originally numeric, only centering was applied to mitigate collinearity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harrison et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.
Models were estimated with REML using the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lmerTest</span> package and achieved stable convergence.
We excluded participants who selected “Prefer not to say” for the education question, resulting in a final sample of 449 observations included in the mixed-effects models.
Model fit was assessed with AIC/BIC and explained variance (marginal and conditional <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, MuMIn<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            We calculated using the <a href="https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=MuMIn/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">MuMIn</a> package.</span></span></span>).
The results are summarized in <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1. Linear Regression Analysis ‣ 5. Results (H1-H4): Effects of Personalization Types and Autonomy Levels of LLM Agent and Individual Differences on Privacy Concern, Trust and Willingness to Use ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a>.
The near-identical marginal and conditional <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> values indicate that variance was mainly explained by fixed effects, with negligible contribution from the scenario-level random intercept (0 in trust/willingness; 0.02 in concern).
Residual diagnostics showed no major violations of normality or homoscedasticity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We present the results for H1–H4 based on the regression coefficients reported in <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1. Linear Regression Analysis ‣ 5. Results (H1-H4): Effects of Personalization Types and Autonomy Levels of LLM Agent and Individual Differences on Privacy Concern, Trust and Willingness to Use ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a>, and in <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1. Linear Regression Analysis ‣ 5. Results (H1-H4): Effects of Personalization Types and Autonomy Levels of LLM Agent and Individual Differences on Privacy Concern, Trust and Willingness to Use ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a> which summarizes the estimated privacy concerns, trust, and willingness to use the LLM agent across nine experimental conditions.</p>
</div>
