<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Notes: Coefficients from linear mixed-effects model with random intercept for </span><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">scenario</span><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. Standard errors in parentheses. Model fit was evaluated with AIC/BIC and explained variance via marginal and conditional </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (MuMIn).
The negligible gap between marginal and conditional </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> suggests that scenario-level random intercepts explained little additional variance.
</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Significance codes: *</span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.05</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, **</span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.01</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, ***</span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>H1: Personalization Types of LLM Agent Have Significant Effects</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1. Linear Regression Analysis ‣ 5. Results (H1-H4): Effects of Personalization Types and Autonomy Levels of LLM Agent and Individual Differences on Privacy Concern, Trust and Willingness to Use ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a>, personalization types significantly affect user privacy concern, trust and willingness to use.
Specifically, <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">compared to Basic personalization, both No personalization and Privacy-aware personalization have significant negative effects on privacy concerns (supporting H1a) and significant positive effects on trust (supporting H1b) and willingness to use (supporting H1c).</span>
For example, the coefficient for Privacy-aware personalization condition for the privacy concern is <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1.391" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.391</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1.391</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating an estimated decrease of 1.391 points on the 7-point privacy concern scale when users interact with the LLM agent was personalized with consideration of individual privacy preferences, compared to Basic personalization without considering users’ privacy preferences.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>H2: The Effects of Autonomy Levels of LLM Agents Are Not Simply Linear</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The effects of autonomy levels of LLM agents are not simply linear across higher or lower levels, and thus H2a and H2b are not supported. H2c is supported.</span>
More specifically, as compared to No autonomy, LLM agents with Intermediate autonomy have significant negative effects on privacy concern and significant positive effects on trust.
In contrast, Full autonomy showed no significant effects, suggesting that interacting with LLM agents under No autonomy and Full autonomy led to similar levels of privacy concern and trust.
No significant differences in willingness to use were found across the three autonomy levels.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4. </span>H3: Autonomy Moderates the Effects of Personalization</h3>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.04465v1/figures/interact-concern.png" id="S5.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/379;" width="476" height="379" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Privacy concern</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.04465v1/figures/interact-trust.png" id="S5.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/379;" width="476" height="379" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Trust</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.04465v1/figures/interact-willing.png" id="S5.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/379;" width="476" height="379" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S5.F4.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Willingness to use</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F4.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.04465v1/figures/interact-label.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/15;" width="476" height="15" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Significant interaction effects of personalization type and agent autonomy level on (a) privacy concern, (b) trust, and (c) willingness to use. Points represent estimated marginal means from the mixed-effects model, and vertical bars indicate 95% confidence intervals of the estimated ratings. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We tested the interaction effects of personalization type and autonomy level together with their main effects, in the same three linear mixed-effects regression models.
As shown in <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.4. H3: Autonomy Moderates the Effects of Personalization ‣ 5. Results (H1-H4): Effects of Personalization Types and Autonomy Levels of LLM Agent and Individual Differences on Privacy Concern, Trust and Willingness to Use ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 4</span></a>, the effects of personalization were moderated by autonomy.
Across all three autonomy levels, the same general trend was observed: when the LLM agent used Basic personalization, users reported the highest privacy concern and the lowest trust and willingness to use.
Full autonomy and No autonomy showed similar patterns, whereas the slope for Intermediate autonomy was smoother than the other two.
<span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">This suggests that Intermediate autonomy flattens the effects of personalization, supporting H3.</span>
A moderation effect of autonomy was also observed in the underlying psychological process detailed in <a href="#S6" title="6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5. </span>H4: Main Effects of Individual Differences</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H4a is partially supported</span>: Users with higher AI literacy reported more trust (Coef. = 0.366, <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>) and greater willingness to use (Coef. = 0.516, <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>) the LLM agent.
<span id="S5.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H4b is partially supported</span>: Higher personal agency was associated with lower privacy concern (Coef. = -0.214, <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.05</annotation></semantics></math>).
<span id="S5.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H4c is not supported</span>: No significant effects were found for interpersonal agency.
<span id="S5.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H4d is partially supported</span>: Female participants, compared to male participants, reported higher privacy concern (Coef. = 0.342, <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.05</annotation></semantics></math>).
Significantly lower trust and willingness to use were found among the Non-binary/third gender group, while this result should be interpreted with caution given the very small sample size in this group (<math id="S5.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math>), which may limit statistical reliability and introduce bias.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness</h2>

<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.04465v1/figures/heatmap_sensitivity.png" id="S6.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/369;" width="476" height="369" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S6.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Perceived Sensitivity:
<br class="ltx_break">(Percentage of participants who answered “Yes” in the question: “Do you think the responses generated by this AI agent contained any sensitive information?”)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.04465v1/figures/heatmap_control.png" id="S6.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/369;" width="476" height="369" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S6.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Perceived Control
<br class="ltx_break">(Percentage of participants who perceived control towards the LLM agent: the mean of perceived control questions is higher than 3)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.04465v1/figures/heatmap_usefulness.png" id="S6.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/369;" width="476" height="369" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S6.F5.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Perceived Usefulness
<br class="ltx_break">(Percentage of participants who perceived the LLM agent is usefulness: the mean of perceived usefulness questions is higher than 3)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S6.F5.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.04465v1/figures/heatmap_label_mediators.png" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/2148;" width="476" height="2148" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>. </span><span id="S6.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Estimates of the percentage of people who (a) perceived sensitivity (answered “Yes” in the question about whether they thought LLM agent’s responses contained any sensitive information), (b) perceived control (mean control rating &gt; 3), and (c) perceived usefulness (mean usefulness rating &gt; 3) across nine experimental conditions (3 personalization types <math id="S6.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 3 autonomy levels).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We calculated the estimates of the three mediator variables (perceived sensitivity, perceived control and perceived usefulness) across difference conditions.
Results are shown in <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Perceived sensitivity.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On average, 63% (94/150) of participants in the Basic personalization condition thought that the LLM agent’s generated responses contained sensitive information.
Among these who perceived sensitive information and had a opportunity to edit the responses, 89% (Intermediate autonomy: 28/34; No autonomy: 30/31) removed at least one piece of sensitive information.
However, all participants in the Basic personalization condition actually encountered at least one sensitive item in the LLM agent’s generated responses according to their self-specified private items (<a href="#S4.SS4.SSS2" title="4.4.2. Validation Checks ‣ 4.4. Data Collection and Participants ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 4.4.2</span></a>).
This indicates that 37% of participants in the Basic personalization” condition overlooked privacy leakage, even though the sensitive items were ones they had explicitly defined as information they did not wish to disclose.
In contrast, 4–6% of participants in the No personalization and Privacy-aware personalization conditions perceived the presence of sensitive information in the generated responses, suggesting they identified other potential leakages beyond the items they had predefined.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Perceived control.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S6.F5.sf2" title="Figure 5(b) ‣ Figure 5 ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a>, the highest proportion of participants (86%) perceived control over the LLM agent in the two No autonomy conditions combined with No personalization and Privacy-aware personalization, where users could always edit and send the messages themselves, even though no sensitive information was involved in the generated responses.
In contrast, the lowest proportion of participants (42%) perceived control in the Full autonomy <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> Basic personalization condition, where users could not exercise any control during the discussion, despite sensitive information being included in the generated responses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Perceived usefulness.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Privacy-aware personalization had the highest proportion of participants perceiving the LLM agent as useful (89%), compared to No personalization (86%) and Basic personalization (81%).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Mediation Analysis</h3>

<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04465v1/figures/model_structure.png" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/162;" width="405" height="162" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>. </span><span id="S6.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Moderated mediation model tested for H5 and H6.
Personalization (No personalization, Privacy-aware personalization; Basic personalization as reference) is modeled as <math id="S6.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
Autonomy (Intermediate autonomy and Full autonomy; No autonomy as reference) as <math id="S6.F6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>,
and their interaction (<math id="S6.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times W</annotation></semantics></math>).
Perceived Sensitivity (M1), Perceived Control (M2), and Perceived Usefulness (M3) serve as mediators,
with Privacy Concern, Trust, and Willingness to Use as outcomes (<math id="S6.F6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>).
The model tests both mediation pathways (<math id="S6.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\times b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\times b</annotation></semantics></math>) and the moderation of personalization effects by autonomy.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted a mediation analysis using structural equation modeling (SEM) to test H5 and H6 about the mediation effect of users’ perceived sensitivity, perceived control and perceived usefulness.
In our mediation analysis, we focused on the relationships that showed significant effects in our linear mixed-effects regression model <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1. Linear Regression Analysis ‣ 5. Results (H1-H4): Effects of Personalization Types and Autonomy Levels of LLM Agent and Individual Differences on Privacy Concern, Trust and Willingness to Use ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a>: the effects of No personalization and Privacy-aware personalization on privacy concern, trust, and willingness to use; and the effects of Intermediate autonomy on privacy concern and trust.
Because the mixed-effects regression also revealed a significant moderating effect of autonomy on personalization, we specified <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a moderated mediation model</span> (see <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 6</span></a>).
In this model, personalization (No personalization and Privacy-aware personalization, with Basic as reference) served as independent variables.
Autonomy was dummy-coded into Intermediate autonomy and Full autonomy (with No autonomy as reference).
Both autonomy dummies were included in the SEM to avoid collapsing Full autonomy into No autonomy, even though Full autonomy did not show significant effects in the mixed-effects regression.
Autonomy was modeled both as (1) an independent variable influencing mediators and outcomes (privacy concern and trust, but not willingness to use), and (2) a moderator of the effects of personalization.
The moderated-mediation SEM fit the data well: <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}(1)=0.314" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.314</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}(1)=0.314</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.854" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.854</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.854</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating no significant misfit.
The Comparative Fit Index (CFI=1.000), Standardized Root Mean Squared Residual (SRMR=0.001) and the Root Mean Square Error of Approximation (RMSEA=0.000) confirmed close fit.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S6.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S6.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Path coefficients from personalization to mediators (<math id="S6.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>), and from mediators to outcomes (<math id="S6.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>).
M1 = perceived sensitivity, M2 = perceived control, M3 = perceived usefulness.
All coefficients unstandardized; 95% CIs in brackets; significant results in bold.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S6.T2.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.T2.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T2.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="S6.T2.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S6.T2.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:69.0pt;">
<span id="S6.T2.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_right">Perceived Sensitivity</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S6.T2.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S6.T2.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:69.0pt;">
<span id="S6.T2.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_right">Perceived Control</span>
</span></td>
