<table id="S6.T4.st4.8" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.T4.st4.8.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T4.st4.8.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S6.T4.st4.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Path</span></th>
<td id="S6.T4.st4.8.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S6.T4.st4.8.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Est.</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T4.st4.8.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T4.st4.8.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S6.T4.st4.8.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">–</span></th>
<td id="S6.T4.st4.8.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T4.st4.8.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T4.st4.8.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><math id="S6.T4.st4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to Y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">W</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Y</mi><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to Y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S6.T4.st4.8.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S6.T4.st4.8.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">–0.65 [–1.32, 0.02]</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T4.st4.8.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T4.st4.8.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><math id="S6.T4.st4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to Y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">W</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Y</mi><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to Y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S6.T4.st4.8.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S6.T4.st4.8.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.16 [–0.14, 0.44]</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T4.st4.8.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T4.st4.8.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S6.T4.st4.8.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">N/A</span></th>
<td id="S6.T4.st4.8.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S6.T4.6" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:341.6pt;"><span id="S6.T4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Notes.</span><span id="S6.T4.6.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> The direct path from Intermediate Autonomy to willingness to use is not included, as no significant main effect of Intermediate Autonomy on willingness to use was found in the linear mixed-effects regression model (</span><a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1. Linear Regression Analysis ‣ 5. Results (H1-H4): Effects of Personalization Types and Autonomy Levels of LLM Agent and Individual Differences on Privacy Concern, Trust and Willingness to Use ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a><span id="S6.T4.6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">).</span></p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results of the moderated-mediation analysis are shown in <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 2</span></a>, which reports the path coefficients from personalization to mediators and from mediators to outcomes; in <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a>, which presents the conditional indirect and direct effects under different autonomy levels; and in <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>, which presents the effects of intermediate autonomy on mediators, outcomes, and indirect pathways.
Referring to <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a> and <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>, only one direct effect was significant: no personalization was associated with less privacy concern (<math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}_{1}=-0.92" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.92</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}_{1}=-0.92</annotation></semantics></math>).
<span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">All other direct effects from personalization and intermediate autonomy to outcomes were nonsignificant, suggesting that the effects of both factors were largely explained through the mediators.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>H5a &amp; H6a: Mediation Effects of Perceived Sensitivity.</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 2</span></a>, both No personalization and Privacy-aware personalization significantly reduced perceived sensitivity, which in turn increased privacy concern (<math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1concern}=1.20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1concern}=1.20</annotation></semantics></math>), decreased trust (<math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1trust}=-0.62" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.62</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1trust}=-0.62</annotation></semantics></math>), and also decreased willingness to use (<math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1willingness}=-0.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.42</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1willingness}=-0.42</annotation></semantics></math>).
Correspondingly, <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a> shows significant indirect effects of both personalization types on privacy concern, trust, and willingness via perceived sensitivity.
Thus, <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H5a is fully supported: personalization shapes users’ privacy concern, trust, and willingness to use through perceptions of sensitivity.</span>
By contrast, as shown in <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>, <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">intermediate autonomy had no significant effect on perceived sensitivity or its downstream outcomes. H6a is therefore not supported.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3. </span>H5b &amp; H6b: Mediation Effects of Perceived Control.</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Personalization also significantly increased perceived control (<math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2no}=0.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.67</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2no}=0.67</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2privacy}=0.66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.66</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2privacy}=0.66</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 2</span></a>), which was negatively associated with privacy concern and positively associated with trust and willingness.
As shown in <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a>, <span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">in the no autonomy condition, both No personalization and Privacy-aware personalization had significant indirect effects via control on all three outcomes (concern, trust, and willingness).
However, these mediation effects became nonsignificant in the intermediate autonomy condition.</span>
For example, the indirect effect of No personalization on trust through perceived control dropped from 0.42 at no autonomy to 0.17 at intermediate autonomy, with a significant index of moderated mediation (–0.25 [–0.46, –0.05]).
This pattern suggests that intermediate autonomy moderates the mediating role of control, such that the pathway is only active under no autonomy. <span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H5b is partially supported.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to moderating these mediation effects, <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a> shows that <span id="S6.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">intermediate autonomy directly increased perceived control (<math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{w2}=0.48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{w2}=0.48</annotation></semantics></math>) and indirectly influenced all three outcomes through control (–0.23 on concern, +0.20 on trust, +0.21 on willingness).
H6b is thus supported.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4. </span>H5c &amp; H6c: Mediation Effects of Perceived Usefulness.</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 2</span></a>, compared with basic personalization, privacy-aware personalization significantly increased perceived usefulness (<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{3privacy}=0.46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.46</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{3privacy}=0.46</annotation></semantics></math>), while no personalization did not (<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{3no}=0.29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.29</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{3no}=0.29</annotation></semantics></math>, n.s.).
Perceived usefulness was strongly associated with trust (<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{3trust}=0.62" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.62</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{3trust}=0.62</annotation></semantics></math>) and willingness (<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{3will}=1.29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.29</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{3will}=1.29</annotation></semantics></math>), and also slightly negatively associated with privacy concern (<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{3concern}=-0.24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.24</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{3concern}=-0.24</annotation></semantics></math>).
In the no autonomy condition, privacy-aware personalization significantly influenced trust and willingness via perceived usefulness, and no personalization significantly influenced willingness.
However, these indirect effects became nonsignificant in the intermediate autonomy condition, with significant indices of moderated mediation.
These findings indicate that <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">perceived usefulness mediated personalization effects on trust and willingness only under no autonomy. H5c is thus partially supported.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>, <span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">intermediate autonomy did not significantly increase perceived usefulness directly (<math id="S6.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{w3}=0.32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{w3}=0.32</annotation></semantics></math>, n.s.), nor did it indirectly influenced privacy concern, trust and willingness through usefulness. H6c is therefore not supported.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we draw on the study results to first discuss how LLM agent autonomy reshapes the Personalization-Privacy Dilemma (<a href="#S7.SS1" title="7.1. Personalization-Privacy Paradox in LLM Agents: Autonomy Matters! ‣ 7. Discussion ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 7.1</span></a>).
We then discuss how to design LLM agent autonomy for trustworthy LLM agent systems by focusing on how autonomy supports human control that aligns with user expectations to mitigate privacy concerns and build trust (<a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2. Designing LLM Agent Autonomy for Human Control and Trust ‣ 7. Discussion ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 7.2</span></a>), and how it can enable effective human oversight to mitigate privacy risks (<a href="#S7.SS3" title="7.3. Enhance Human Oversight for Mitigating Privacy Risks ‣ 7. Discussion ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 7.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1. </span>Personalization-Privacy Paradox in LLM Agents: Autonomy Matters!</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior studies on the personalization-privacy paradox have largely focused on non-agentic AI systems such as recommender systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karwatzki et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aguirre et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cloarec, 2020</a>)</cite>, where system actions are limited to generating content outputs and user privacy concerns primarily arise from institutional data collection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prunkl, 2024</a>)</cite>.
Our study extends this paradigm to LLM agents, which differ from non-agentic systems by endowing varying levels of autonomy in agents’ actions.
It introduces novel privacy risks and privacy concerns.
On one hand, large language models are poor at aligning with individual privacy preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, and when autonomy is granted, these vulnerabilities can extend into the environments the agent interacts with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
On the other hand, granting action autonomy inherently entails a relinquishing of human control, heightening users’ sensitivity to privacy risks.
Such concerns move beyond institutional data practices to <span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">interpersonal and contextual risks</span>.
For example, an autonomous agent used for interpersonal communication may inadvertently disclose third-party information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, while in professional settings it may raise concerns about confidentiality or reputational damage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, thereby posing new challenges for user trust in agentic AI systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raheem and Hossain, 2025</a>)</cite>.
In our results, we observed a pattern of personalization effects (<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. H1: Personalization Types of LLM Agent Have Significant Effects ‣ 5. Results (H1-H4): Effects of Personalization Types and Autonomy Levels of LLM Agent and Individual Differences on Privacy Concern, Trust and Willingness to Use ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 5.2</span></a>) similar to prior studies: personalization without considering user privacy preferences increased privacy concerns and reduced trust and willingness to use, whereas personalization that considered privacy preferences reduced privacy concerns and enhanced trust and willingness to use.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, our findings also revealed that Intermediate autonomy of an LLM agent flattened the effects of personalization (<a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. H3: Autonomy Moderates the Effects of Personalization ‣ 5. Results (H1-H4): Effects of Personalization Types and Autonomy Levels of LLM Agent and Individual Differences on Privacy Concern, Trust and Willingness to Use ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 5.4</span></a>), indicating that autonomy fundamentally shapes how acceptable personalization feels to users.
This suggests the central role of autonomy in addressing the personalization-privacy paradox for LLM agents, and that the effects of personalization cannot be understood in isolation from autonomy.
The HCI and AI communities have invested significant effort in <span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">model alignment</span>, aiming to ensure that model outputs content aligns with human values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gabriel, 2020</a>)</cite>, including privacy preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shaikh et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, thereby mitigating privacy concerns and fostering trust <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirk et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
<span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our study suggests that, rather than solely pursuing perfect model-output alignment, balancing agent autonomy with user control offers a promising alternative for addressing the personalization-privacy dilemma, enabling users to benefit from more customized experiences without being deterred by growing privacy concerns.</span>
This highlights a complementary axis of alignment beyond output content: <span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the alignment of autonomy</span>.
This concerns the boundaries of when agents should (and should not) act autonomously on behalf of users, and how users retain or exercise control.
In this sense, autonomy alignment becomes a key determinant of whether personalization is perceived as beneficial or risky.
Our results resonate with emerging views of multiple types of alignment in LLM agent systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which extend beyond traditional output alignment to include autonomy and agency alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2. </span>Designing LLM Agent Autonomy for Human Control and Trust</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Delegate user control when <em id="S7.SS2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">necessary</em>.</span>
Interestingly, users interacting with Intermediate autonomy agents reported greater perceived control, lower privacy concerns, and higher trust and willingness compared to those with No autonomy agents (<a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a>).
This seems paradoxical, as users in the No autonomy condition technically held more control: they always reviewed and confirmed whether to send the agent’s responses.
Similar patterns have been observed in prior work, where requiring constant user approval produced decision fatigue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Echterhoff et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahmed and Pardaev, 2025</a>)</cite> and improper delegation design reduced sense of agency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steyvers and Kumar, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zanatto et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, as responsibility was offloaded onto users rather than supported by the system.
In contrast, Intermediate autonomy delegated control only when potential privacy risks were detected, aligning better with users’ actual needs.
This suggests that effective autonomy design requires carefully selecting the “delegation moments” that align with user expectations and risk perceptions, rather than maximizing oversight in every step.
Moreover, these “delegation moments” could be further personalized: different contexts may involve different categories of risk (e.g., financial disclosure in family discussions versus reputational concerns in professional settings), and individuals vary in their sensitivity to these risks.
Our individual-differences results suggest that people with different traits (e.g., higher or lower AI literacy) may hold different boundaries of acceptable personalization and autonomy, highlighting the potential for autonomy design that adapts to both situational and personal factors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Design LLM agent autonomy informed by the underlying psychological processes.</span>
Current LLM agent designs of autonomy have explored multiple approaches but lack systematic guidelines.
For example, Operator asks users for confirmation when pre-defined sensitive domains such as finance or health are involved <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025b</a>)</cite>, while Cursor asks users at the beginning of a session to choose the level of oversight they prefer (always confirm vs. execute automatically) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cursor Documentation, 2025</a>)</cite>.
Our moderated mediation analysis (<a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.1. Mediation Analysis ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a>) reveal the psychological pathways through which the autonomy level and personalization collectively influence users’ trust, privacy concerns, and willingness, which can provide guidance for LLM agent design.
For example, if the design goal is to improve user’s willingness to use, perceived control and usefulness can be used as additional signals to guide how the autonomy and personalization should be co-designed, as we found these variables to be the significant mediators.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3. </span>Enhance Human Oversight for Mitigating Privacy Risks</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to designing agent autonomy to improve subjective perceptions as discussed in <a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2. Designing LLM Agent Autonomy for Human Control and Trust ‣ 7. Discussion ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 7.2</span></a> (e.g., reducing privacy concerns and increasing trust), we emphasize the importance of supporting effective human oversight of privacy leakage.
As shown in <a href="#S6.F5.sf1" title="Figure 5(a) ‣ Figure 5 ‣ 6. Results (H5-H6): Mediation Effect of Perceived Sensitivity, Perceived Control and Perceived Usefulness ‣ Autonomy Matters: A Study on Personalization-Privacy Dilemma in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a>, only 62.7% of participants in Basic personalization condition recognized that the LLM agent’s responses contained information that they had just reported as sensitive and wanted to keep private. This means that even if the remaining 37.3% were given privacy control, they would fail to identify privacy leakage and such control mechanisms can fall short in practice.
Notably, the rate of sensitive-information recognition improved in the Intermediate autonomy condition compared with both Full and No autonomy. This further shows that Intermediate autonomy, which features a pre-selection mechanism that interrupts users only when sensitivity is likely, not only enhances subjective perceptions but also improves objective oversight efficacy.
In this way, Intermediate autonomy provides an opportunity to gather meaningful user feedback (e.g., through user oversight behaviors), which can then support model personalization approaches such as reinforcement learning.
Achieving perfect model alignment is far from straightforward, because privacy preferences are highly subjective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee and Weber, 2025</a>)</cite>, contextual <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, dynamic and subject to change over time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldfarb and Tucker, 2012</a>)</cite>, and often difficult to elicit reliably <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.
These autonomy-supported feedback approaches may therefore be more practical than aiming for static, perfect alignment with individual users, while still moving systems closer to what users expect.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we take the first step to study how different personalization and autonomy levels in LLM agents affect users’ privacy concerns, trust, willingness to use, and the underlying psychological processes.
We conducted a 3<math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>3 between-participants experiment (<math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=450" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=450</annotation></semantics></math>), randomly assigning each participant to one condition where an LLM agent acted on their behalf in an interpersonal communication scenario.
Our results show that personalization without considering users’ privacy preferences increases privacy concerns and decreases trust and willingness to use.
Unlike prior non-agentic AI systems, intermediate autonomy in an agent’s actions attenuates these personalization effects by moderating the mediators in the underlying psychological process.
Rather than relying solely on perfect model alignment, our findings point to a promising path through designing appropriate levels of autonomy in LLM agents that support user control, thereby mitigating privacy concerns and improving trust and willingness to use.</p>
</div>
</section>
